  --- pcprobe, 120000 ticks ---
experiment: pcprobe
  session           120.0 s, caregiver speaks 30% of it, asleep 0%
  predict_gain      0.00   (off — this run measures the model, not the effect)
  frames scored     7498

  Is the prediction worth subtracting?
                                all     loud    quiet
    R^2 vs channel mean       0.636    0.056   -1.820
    R^2 of persistence        0.899    0.791    0.524   <- the baseline that matters
    corr(pred, prev frame)    0.626    0.631    0.467
    sd(pred)/sd(mel)          0.795    1.039    0.769
    spectral shape corr       0.963
    R^2 first half / second    0.633 -> 0.633   (persistence 0.894 -> 0.902)

    R^2 at or below zero means the forward model does no better than
    predicting each channel's own average. Beating that is necessary and
    nowhere near sufficient: persistence — "the next frame looks like this
    one" — costs nothing and scores well on a spectrum that changes over
    a syllable. The model has to beat *that* row to be a model, and if
    corr(pred, prev frame) is near 1 it has simply learned to echo.
    "quiet" is the reafference test: the only sound then is the
    creature's own voice, driven by the very module the model reads.

  What the subtraction costs
    mean channel energy      0.0894 -> 0.0894  (100% kept)
    cells rectified away       0.0%
    ...at gain 1.0            37.8%   (what full subtraction would clip)
    B2 mean rate               4.37 Hz

  Ceiling: the best a linear readout could do (7498 rows, held out)
    critic's 32 bins, fitted offline      0.005
    every central neuron                 -0.071
    previous frame alone                  0.917
    central + previous frame              0.889
    central, rows shuffled               -0.168   (control: must be <= 0)
    central -> current frame             -0.056   (control: the frame it is hearing NOW)
    B2 -> current frame                   0.525
    B2 -> next frame                      0.469
    target from a copy of itself          1.000   (self-test: must be ~1)

  the same, with the target decorrelated (13 MFCCs, DCT of the log-mel)
    MFCC from a copy of itself            1.000   (self-test: must be ~1)
    central -> current frame             -0.088
    central -> next frame                -0.095
    previous frame alone                  0.903

    Read down the first two rows for the pooling: if per-neuron beats
    32 bins the critic is throwing the signal away before it fits, and
    kCriticBins is the repair. Read the last two against "previous frame
    alone" for the harder question — whether the association module knows
    anything about the next sound that the last sound does not already say.
  [pcprobe took 2.7 s]

