    applying the aim twice CONVERGES: the loop lands 0.0 px from the
    toy while the host reports 0.0 px, because each end is doing half
    the aiming and only one of them is being asked. Nothing above
    fails; the telemetry, the journal and every gaze number are wrong.

    a silent eye costs 0.0 px of command drift with the freeze on and
    0.0 px with it off, because by the time the link drops the eye is
    already on the toy and there is no error left to act on.
    with the eye lost on the FIRST frame, before it ever gets there:
    0.0 px held against 0.0 px open. That is the whole of it, and it
    is bounded by construction rather than by the freeze — the loop is
    proportional with no integrator, so a command is one gain-step off
    a frozen belief and cannot wind up however long the device is gone.
    The freeze buys that step and an explicit `held` counter. The
    runaway it was built against needs a device that answers LATE, not
    one that stops: that is the servo row, at 26 px.

  THE EYE NEVER MOVED — the reflex rows are the fixed rows under
  another name. Nothing above is a measurement of a controller.

  ACQUISITION: where does the eye end up, per starting offset
    the fovea spans +/-8 px; ring 1 +/-16 px; ring 2 +/-32 px
    one toy, fixed size, eye released from the centre and given 40
    frames to converge. The first version of this read the gaze after a
    SINGLE frame and reported the eye blind at 2 px, which cannot be
    true because the reflex works inside the fovea — the command goes
    through the eye's queue and is not visible that soon.

