  gain is what the tract DELIVERED as a fraction of its genome weight.
  transfer is central's spikes per auditory spike — a ratio, because the
  ear's own response to an envelope is not flat and a count at central
  would mostly be measuring that. `vs off` is the same ratio against the
  off arm's row for the SAME envelope, and it is where an envelope filter
  would show up: a gain change is flat down that column and a filter is
  not.

  off arm gain           1.0000   (must be 1.0000 — the control)
  depressing, worst      0.719   (must be under 0.950)
  facilitating, best     1.599   (must be over 1.050)
  rate/gain corr, dep.   -0.936   (must be under -0.500)

  PASS — the mechanism runs, the two corners do opposite things, and
  what each delivers tracks the traffic it carried.

  WHAT IT BUYS HERE, read off the `vs off` column:
  nothing that a volume knob would not. `vs off` is FLAT across the
  envelopes — a filter would peak somewhere and it does not.

  The reason is NOT that the ear is regulated flat. It is not: the
  module goes 0.53 spikes per tick in silence to 1.34 under speech,
  +153%, and `ipprobe` measures the same thing independently. An
  earlier version of this probe settled for only 1500 ticks and read
  silence on a just-hatched creature, which made that gap look like
  3% and supported an explanation that was wrong.

  The real reason is that ONE dynamic synapse cannot be a bandpass.
  Depression scales everything this synapse transmits by a single
  number that follows its own mean rate, so it is a high-pass with no
  upper corner: `gain` tracks the traffic faithfully and every spike
  gets the same multiplier. Webb's bandpass is TWO stages — BN1
  depressing, feeding BN2 facilitating — and the tuning lives in the
  mismatch between their time constants, not in either one. This
  genome can express that today: a relay module between auditory and
  central, depressing on the way in and facilitating on the way out.
  It has not been built.
  [stpprobe took 3.5 s]

