  EXCESS in NEAREST-TARGET FRACTION over each encoding's OWN matched
  marginal, paired on seed. The fraction rather than the margin, because the
  margin rewards COLLINEAR targets about 2x for the same learning -- see the
  note above; gating on it made a metric artefact read as an 8.4 SE effect.
    collide  +0.4167 +/- 0.0732  (+5.7 SE)
    ortho    +0.2778 +/- 0.0730  (+3.8 SE)
    ortho - collide  -0.1389 +/- 0.0926  (-1.5 SE)  [paired]

  THE VOCABULARY MAKES NO DIFFERENCE (-1.5 SE on the geometry-free
  measure). Spreading the two words across articulators names neither better
  nor worse than stacking them on F1, so `orthovocab`'s collision result is a
  fact about SEQUENTIAL lessons and does not transfer to simultaneous
  conditional ones. Interference between lessons taught one after another,
  and the failure to condition on the heard word, are different problems.

  BUT READ THE ABSOLUTE NUMBERS BEFORE CALLING THIS A NULL: both encodings
  name, and well -- nearest-target 0.958 against matched marginals at 0.389
  and 0.542, roughly 16 SE of excess each. With contexts on, this creature
  DOES say the right thing for the word it heard.

  AND DO NOT READ THAT AS THE MILESTONE. These targets sit 0.29-0.41 log
  units from rest and are SYMMETRIC about it; the shipped vowel vocabulary is
  0.89 apart and asymmetric, demanding a 0.67 move on one word alone. Naming
  works here because the required travel is small -- which is exactly what
  `ceiling-is-an-exponent` says: the wall is how FAR reward can move a
  formant, not whether it can steer conditionally. The right next question is
  where between 0.58 and 0.89 the score falls off.
