  ONE NEURON OF 14 COSTS 30% OF THE LESSON, against 53% for four --
  a steeply COMPRESSIVE cost, not a proportional one and not the flat one
  `compartprobe` reported over 25-50% (that ladder sat on the plateau).

  DO NOT READ THIS AS A FAULTY INSTRUMENT. `set_reward_block` skips exactly
  the neurons in its range and has no global effect -- it is not
  `set_reward_mask`, whose inverted condition froze the whole network. The
  likelier account is LEVERAGE: the block always takes the TOP of the
  group, the centroid weights neuron i by (i - begin + 0.5)/n, so the
  blocked cells are the high-F1 end and the best levers for moving the
  readout. The block removes the best lever first, which is exactly a
  steep-then-saturating curve.

  WHAT SEPARATES THE TWO is WHERE the block sits, not how big it is: one
  neuron at the BOTTOM or the MIDDLE, same count, different leverage. If
  position matters the instrument is fine and the readout's geometry is
  the finding; if it does not, the instrument is back under suspicion.
  [blockfloor took 4267.9 s]

