    `deaf-to-itself` (5 creatures, ~60 genome versions ago, self_gain
    0 / 0.5 / 2.0):  amplitude duty 0.84 / 0.67 / 0.39, falling --
    "louder self-hearing makes the creature quieter, monotonically".
    measured here (12 creatures):  0.480 / 0.461 / 0.347
    the recorded DIRECTION reproduces at this length -- falling, so the loop
    is still net negative feedback. The MAGNITUDES are stale: the deaf end
    reads 0.480 against a recorded 0.84 and the loud end 0.347 against 0.39, so
    the SPAN has collapsed from 0.45 to 0.134. Quote the sign, not the values.
    AND NOTE THE LENGTH: at 60k ticks this ordering RISES, and so does
    `babble` at its own 120k. Intrinsic plasticity needs time to raise
    thresholds, so a short run sees only the excitatory leg of the loop.

  --- the primary, against a number fixed before the run ---
    predicted mean voiced stretch  172 ms   (from drift = gain * sigma * T)
    measured                       176.6 ms   ratio 1.03
    THE ACCOUNT HOLDS within a factor of two. The ungated schedule's drift
    floor IS set by how long F1 integrates between silences, which is why
    shortening its anchor past that point bought nothing.

  --- is it a RHYTHM or just a schedule? ---
    interval CV   voiced 0.74   unvoiced 1.05   (1.0 = memoryless, bar 0.70)
    implied gate rate 2.99 Hz   spectral peak 2.12 Hz at snr 5.8
    margins: voiced CV is 0.04 from the bar, unvoiced 0.35 -- the UNVOICED
    intervals are what decide it, and the voiced side sits ON the bar: claim nothing from it.
    rate agreement: intervals 2.99 Hz vs spectral peak 2.12 Hz, 29% apart at snr 5.8
    THEY DISAGREE by more than a fifth. The interval rate is the primary --
    the peak is a scanned argmax and this project has already lost a result
    to one. Do not quote the peak as the gate's frequency.
    A SCHEDULE IN THE RIGHT BAND, AND NOT A RHYTHM. The mean rate is 2.99 Hz
    -- syllable-scale -- but the intervals are near-memoryless (CV 0.74), so
    there is no clock here. That is consistent with everything: an irregular
    schedule can anchor an integrator perfectly well and still carry no
    timing, which is exactly what beat the jaw.
